--TEST--
an attribute payload's every field value is a literal, a class constant or an enum case, and anything the program would have to run is refused where it is written — `rule:attributes/attach-sites-and-forms` and `rule:attributes/payload-is-a-compile-time-constant`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:attributes/an-attribute-is-an-anonymous-object-attached-to-a-declaration
// `rule:attributes/payload-is-a-compile-time-constant`: the whole payload is resolved once, at compile time, into the
// unit's constant pool — the storage class an enum case's backing integer and
// a class constant already use. There is therefore no moment at which a
// variable could be read, a call made or a `new` constructed, which is exactly
// the moment PHP's attributes do have (`ReflectionAttribute::newInstance()`
// builds a real object lazily) and this ADR deliberately does not.
enum Mode: int {
    Read = 1,
    Write = 2,
}

class Limits {
    public const int MAX = 255;
}

// `rule:attributes/attach-sites-and-forms`: a named attribute's name is a shape-typed `type` alias and
// nothing else, so every name written below is declared here. What each
// payload may *hold* is § 2's rule and is the same either way.
type Route = {path: string};
type Audit = {};
type Column = {};
type Cache = {};
type Autowire = {};

// The accepted half first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal. `rule:attributes/attach-sites-and-forms`'s two attach forms are both
// here — the named `Name(field: value)` and the bare `{field: value}` — since
// naming is attach-site sugar and never changes what a payload may hold.
#[Route(path: "/users", method: "GET"), {tag: 1}]
#[Audit]
class Accepted {
    #[Column(
        length: Limits::MAX,
        mode: Mode::Read,
        offset: -3,
        ratio: 1.5,
        nullable: true,
        absent: null,
        flags: [1, 2],
        nested: {deep: Limits::MAX},
    )]
    public string $name = "n";
}

// A variable is the shape the rule exists for: there is no frame for it to be
// read out of at the point the payload is written into the pool.
string $host = "example.com";

#[Route(path: $host)]
class Refused {
    // A call and a `new` are the other two § 2 names, and each is reported at
    // the field it is written in rather than at the attribute, so a payload
    // with two bad fields reads as two mistakes.
    #[Column(length: Core\Str::length("ab"), other: new Refused())]
    public string $field = "f";

    // An interpolated string reads a variable by definition, whatever it
    // interpolates, so it is not the string-literal row one syntax along.
    #[Cache(key: "host-$host")]
    public function show(#[Autowire(from: $host)] int $id): int {
        return $id;
    }
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0725]: an attribute's field value is not a compile-time constant
   --> case.nvs:51:15
